+{id:'c2',ch:'code',type:'w',
+ q:'Describe the TimerState dataclass changes in a20fbb1: what was removed, what was added, what the new comments document, and what PERIOD? returns after *RST as a result.',
+ c:[{l:'removed: period: int = 0xFFFF',t:['period','0xffff','removed','deleted']},
+    {l:'added: cc_mode dict, all four units defaulting to COMPARE',t:['cc_mode','compare','dict','default']},
+    {l:'comments: PERIOD is not a separate register (CCR0 alias); CAP is stored state only, time does not pass',t:['not a separate','alias','stored state','time does not pass','§25.2.3.1','25.2.4']},
+    {l:'*RST rebuilds TimerState() → PERIOD? now returns 0 (CCR0 reset value)',t:['*rst','returns 0','reset value','ccr0','zero']}],
+ m:`Removed: the field "period: int = 0xFFFF" — the phantom second store that let PERIOD and CC0:VALUE diverge.
+Added: cc_mode: Dict[int, str] defaulting to {0..3: "COMPARE"} — the per-CC-unit CAP bit as real stored state.
+The new comments are load-bearing documentation: above capture_compare, that PERIOD is NOT a separate register — in up mode the period register IS TAxCCR0 (§25.2.3.1), so TIMERn:PERIOD reads/writes capture_compare[0] directly; above cc_mode, that the CAP bit is stored state only, with no edge-triggered capture latching "since time does not pass in this simulation".
+Because *RST rebuilds every TimerState() from the dataclass defaults, PERIOD? after reset now answers 0 (TAxCCR0's reset value) instead of the old 65535 — a visible behaviour change that is actually a correctness fix, and cc_mode snaps back to COMPARE on every unit.`
+},
