+{id:'b1',ch:'bug',type:'mc',
+ q:'Spot the bug. A pre-a20fbb1 SIL test did this and passed. What was it actually testing?',
+ snip:`TIMERA0:MODE UP
+TIMERA0:PERIOD 1000        <span class="cm"># set the top value</span>
+TIMERA0:PERIOD?            <span class="cm"># → 1000  ✓ assertion passed</span>
+<span class="cm"># firmware's PWM module meanwhile reads CCR0 for its duty math…</span>
+TIMERA0:CC0:VALUE?         <span class="cm"># → 0     (!!)</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Nothing real: PERIOD and CC0 were two independent stores, so the test verified a phantom register while the CCR0 the firmware depends on still held 0',c:true, w:'Correct. The old model round-tripped writes through the standalone period field, so PERIOD? == 1000 proved only that a Python attribute works. On silicon both names are TAxCCR0 — the state the test thought it verified did not exist as tested. After the fix both queries return 1000.'},
+  {t:'The test forgot to start the timer, so the period write was ignored',c:false,w:'Writes are never gated on the timer running, and nothing was ignored — the value landed, just in a store that hardware doesn\'t have.'},
+  {t:'1000 exceeds the period register\'s width',c:false,w:'1000 fits easily in 16 bits. The defect was aliasing (two stores for one register), not width.'},
+  {t:'UP mode disables CCR0 reads',c:false,w:'No mode disables register reads. CC0:VALUE? returned 0 simply because the PERIOD write never touched capture_compare[0].'},
