+{id:'b4',ch:'bug',type:'w',
+ q:'Firmware configures TIMERA0:CC1:MODE CAPTURE and then busy-waits polling CC1:VALUE? for a nonzero "captured timestamp". In the simulator this hangs forever. Is the simulator wrong? What should a SIL test assert instead?',
+ c:[{l:'not wrong: capture events need edges and time; the sim is stored-state only',t:['stored state','no edge','time does not pass','not simulated','not wrong','by design']},
+    {l:'CCR1 only changes via explicit writes — nothing ever latches TAxR into it',t:['latch','never','only changes','explicit','writes']},
+    {l:'assert configuration: MODE? == CAPTURE (and rejection of bad tokens)',t:['mode?','reads back','configuration','capture']},
+    {l:'dynamic half is out of scope — documented in docstring/audit (PARTIAL); inject state if the flow needs a value',t:['partial','docstring','audit','out of scope','inject']}],
+ m:`The simulator is behaving as designed and documented. Capture events require an input edge and a passing timeline; this simulation has neither — the _timer_cc_mode docstring says "no edge inputs latch TAxCCRn since time does not pass", and the audit scores CC:MODE as PARTIAL for exactly this reason. CCR1 changes only when a SCPI write changes it, so polling for a spontaneous nonzero value waits for an event the model has explicitly declared out of scope.
+A SIL test should assert what stored-state modeling CAN answer: that firmware CONFIGURED the peripheral correctly — CC1:MODE? reads back CAPTURE (the a20fbb1 fix makes this meaningful; before, it read COMPARE and this test would fail spuriously), invalid tokens draw -224, *RST restores COMPARE. If a downstream flow needs a captured value to proceed, the test injects one explicitly (write CC1:VALUE) and asserts the firmware's HANDLING of it — the same pattern as inject_adc for conversions.
+The wrong responses would be: pretending a capture happened (the model would be inventing physics), or the old refusal to store the mode (which made even the configuration check impossible).`
+},
