+<section>
+<h2>2. Encodings Bound Reachable Values — the ID × TAIDEX Worked Example</h2>
+
+<p>Width says a value <em>fits</em>; it does not say a value is <em>reachable</em>. The timer clock divider is the perfect example. There is no "divider register" holding a number like 64. There are two small fields (SLAU367P §25.2.1.1):</p>
+
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>ID</strong> — 2 bits in <code>TAxCTL</code>, selecting a first-stage divide of <strong>1, 2, 4, or 8</strong></li>
+  <li><strong>TAIDEX</strong> — 3 bits in <code>TAxEX0</code>, selecting a second-stage divide of <strong>1 through 8</strong></li>
+</ul>
+
+<p>The effective divider is the <em>product</em> ID × TAIDEX. Enumerate every combination:</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>ID \ TAIDEX</th><th>÷1</th><th>÷2</th><th>÷3</th><th>÷4</th><th>÷5</th><th>÷6</th><th>÷7</th><th>÷8</th></tr>
