+    <span class="add">VALID = {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}</span>        <span class="cm"># AFTER</span>
+    ...
+    <span class="kw">if</span> v <span class="kw">not in</span> VALID:
+        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">ScpiError</span>(-<span class="num">224</span>, f<span class="str">"Illegal divider: {v}"</span>)</code></pre>
+
+<div class="callout warn">
+  <div class="callout-label">⚠ Width and encoding are different constraints</div>
+  <code>128</code> fits comfortably in 16 bits — width has no objection. It is the <em>encoding</em> that rules it out: the divider is not a stored number but a derived product of two small fields. When a SCPI parameter mirrors hardware configuration, the accepted set must be (a subset of) the values the encodings can reach — the same rule that made <code>ADC:REFERENCE BANANA</code> illegal in lesson 0009.
+</div>
+</section>
