+<header>
+  <div class="lesson-label">Vocab 0010 · Silmulator · from issue #19, commit a20fbb1</div>
+  <h1>Register Width and Encoding Constraints — Vocabulary</h1>
+  <p class="tagline">Eight terms behind the timer register fixes. Each has a definition, a project example from the real code, a usage sentence, and a contrast with the term it's most often confused with.</p>
+</header>
+
+<nav class="term-index">
+  <a href="#register-width">register width</a>
+  <a href="#encoding">encoding</a>
+  <a href="#reachable-state-space">reachable state space</a>
+  <a href="#write-masking">write masking</a>
+  <a href="#taidex">TAIDEX</a>
+  <a href="#cap-bit">CAP bit</a>
+  <a href="#register-aliasing">register aliasing</a>
+  <a href="#stored-state-modeling">stored-state modeling</a>
+</nav>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="register-width">
+  <h2>Register width</h2>
+  <div class="pron">16 bits → 0 … 0xFFFF, nothing else</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>The number of physical flip-flops in a hardware register, which fixes its representable values at exactly 0 through 2<sup>width</sup> − 1 (unsigned). Width is a property of the silicon, not of the software modeling it: a value needing more bits does not "overflow" the register — it has no representation in it at all.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>TAxR and TAxCCRn are 16-bit registers (SLAU367P §25.2.1, §25.2.4). Since commit a20fbb1, <code>_timer_count</code> and <code>_timer_cc_val</code> guard writes with <code>if not 0 &lt;= v &lt;= 0xFFFF: raise ScpiError(-222, …)</code>. Before, <code>TIMERA0:COUNT 0x12345</code> stored 74565 in a "16-bit" counter — the audit probe <code>timer_count_over_16bit</code> pinned it.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"That period of 200,000 counts violates the register width — CCR0 is 16 bits, so you need a bigger clock divider, not a bigger number."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: Python int width</dt>
+    <dd>Python's <code>int</code> is arbitrary-precision — it happily holds 0x12345, negative numbers, anything. That is exactly why the model needs an <em>explicit</em> width guard: the modeling language is wider than the modeled hardware in both directions, and every register write must be narrowed by policy (reject, here) rather than by the language.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="encoding">
+  <h2>Encoding</h2>
+  <div class="pron">what a register field's bit patterns mean</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>The finite mapping from a register field's bit patterns to hardware behaviours. An n-bit field has at most 2<sup>n</sup> encodings, so any setting expressed through fields is drawn from a finite menu — there is no bit pattern for "divide by 128" or "reference = 1.8 V" if the menu doesn't list one.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>The timer divider is ID (2 bits: ÷1/2/4/8) × TAIDEX (3 bits: ÷1…8). Commit a20fbb1 removed <code>128</code> from <code>_timer_clk_div</code>'s <code>VALID</code> set because no ID×TAIDEX product reaches it; the write now draws <code>ScpiError(-224, "Illegal divider: 128")</code>. Same rule as lesson 0009's <code>ADC12VRSEL</code>: the accepted set mirrors the encodings.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"128 fits in the register just fine — it's the encoding that rules it out: neither divider field has a 16."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: register width</dt>
+    <dd>Width bounds the <em>magnitude</em> a register can hold (0x10000 fails it → -222). Encoding bounds which values are <em>meaningful/reachable</em> within that magnitude (/128 fits but has no field combination → -224). The divider bug was an encoding violation; the count bug was a width violation.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="reachable-state-space">
+  <h2>Reachable state space</h2>
+  <div class="pron">every state some encoding can actually produce</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>The set of device states that real hardware can occupy — the closure of all register field encodings and their interactions. A simulator's accepted inputs must keep its state inside this set; the moment they don't, the simulation describes a device that cannot exist, and every downstream result is silently void.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>The reachable divider set is the twenty ID×TAIDEX products {1…8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32, 40, 48, 56, 64}. The pre-a20fbb1 model reached 21 states — the extra one, /128, existed only in Python. A SIL test that configured /128 validated timing math for imaginary hardware and passed.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"Before merging a device handler, enumerate the reachable state space and check every accepted parameter lands inside it."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: the model's accepted set</dt>
+    <dd>The accepted set may be a deliberate <em>subset</em> of reachable states — <code>VALID = {1,2,4,8,16,32,64}</code> omits reachable products like /48, a documented simplification that fails loudly with -224. The compliance sin is only the other direction: accepting states <em>outside</em> the reachable space, which fails silently.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="write-masking">
+  <h2>Write masking</h2>
+  <div class="pron">value &amp; 0xFFFF — keep what fits, drop the rest</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>The policy of truncating an over-width write to the register's width (bitwise AND with the width mask) instead of refusing it. It models what a narrow bus does physically — only 16 wires arrive, so only 16 bits land — keeping stored state representable while silently altering the value.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>Silmulator <em>rejects</em> instead (-222), but the flipped test deliberately blesses either policy: <code>assert errors(mcu) == 1 <strong>or</strong> mcu.timer_a[0].count &lt;= 0xFFFF</code> in <code>test_count_over_16bit_rejected_or_masked</code>. What the disjunction forbids is the third option the old model took — storing 0x12345 verbatim.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"Masking 200000 to 0x0D40 gives you a 3.4-ms period instead of 200 ms — hardware-faithful, and precisely the silent failure the SIL run should be diagnosing instead of imitating."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: rejection (-222)</dt>
+    <dd>Both keep the state space honest. Masking reproduces the bench symptom; rejection uses information real hardware never has — the SCPI layer sees the un-truncated intent — to point at the offending write via the error queue. A diagnostic tool wants the loud option; this repo rejects.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="taidex">
+  <h2>TAIDEX</h2>
+  <div class="pron">SLAU367P §25.2.1.1 — the second divider stage</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>The 3-bit input divider expansion field in <code>TAxEX0</code>, dividing the already-ID-divided timer clock by 1 through 8. The effective prescale is the product ID × TAIDEX, which is why the achievable dividers are products of {1,2,4,8} × {1…8} — twenty distinct values topping out at /64.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>The model folds both stages into one SCPI parameter: <code>TIMERA0:CLOCK:DIVIDER</code> with <code>VALID = {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}</code> and the a20fbb1 docstring "ID x TAIDEX, max /64". The deleted <code>128</code> would have required a 16 in a field whose 3 bits stop at 8 — the exact impossibility <code>probe_timer_divider_128</code> now guards.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"/40 is reachable on silicon — ID 8 with TAIDEX 5 — even though the model's power-of-two set doesn't expose it."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: ID</dt>
+    <dd>ID is the first stage: 2 bits in <code>TAxCTL</code>, powers of two only (1/2/4/8). TAIDEX is the second: 3 bits, <em>any</em> integer 1–8, which is what puts non-powers-of-two like /3, /6, /48 into the reachable set. Max divider = max(ID) × max(TAIDEX) = 8 × 8 = 64.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="cap-bit">
+  <h2>CAP bit</h2>
+  <div class="pron">SLAU367P §25.2.4 — capture (1) or compare (0)</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>The per-CC-unit mode bit in <code>TAxCCTLn</code>: in <strong>compare</strong> mode the CCRn register holds a value firmware wrote (PWM edges, periodic events); in <strong>capture</strong> mode an input edge latches the running count <em>into</em> CCRn (timestamping, pulse measurement). One bit flips the register's data direction.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>Before a20fbb1, <code>TIMERA0:CC1:MODE</code> ignored writes and always answered <code>COMPARE</code>. The fix added <code>cc_mode: Dict[int, str]</code> to <code>TimerState</code> and a validated handler: CAPTURE persists and reads back; <code>BANANA</code> draws -224. The docstring scopes it honestly: "no edge inputs latch TAxCCRn since time does not pass".</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"The SIL test can now assert the driver set the CAP bit on CC1 — it still can't wait for a capture, because nothing ever edges."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: capture event</dt>
+    <dd>The CAP bit is <em>configuration</em> — static, storable, resettable by <code>*RST</code>. The capture <em>event</em> is behaviour — it needs CM edge select, CCIS routing, and a passing timeline. a20fbb1 modeled the first fully and documented the second as out of scope; the audit verdict moved NON_COMPLIANT → PARTIAL, not COMPLIANT.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="register-aliasing">
+  <h2>Register aliasing</h2>
+  <div class="pron">one store, two names</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>When two names in an interface denote the <em>same</em> physical register, so a write through either name is visible through both. A faithful model implements an alias as delegation to a single store — never as a second variable, which would let the two names hold contradictory values no silicon can represent.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>In up mode the Timer_A period register IS TAxCCR0 (SLAU367P §25.2.3.1). a20fbb1 deleted the independent <code>period: int = 0xFFFF</code> field and reduced <code>_timer_period</code> to <code>return self._timer_cc_val(cmd, ts, 0)</code> — so <code>TIMERA0:PERIOD 1000</code> then <code>CC0:VALUE?</code> returns 1000, as <code>test_period_is_ccr0_in_up_mode</code> now guards. Side effect: <code>PERIOD?</code> after <code>*RST</code> reads CCR0's reset value, 0, not the phantom 0xFFFF.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"PERIOD is a register alias, not a setting — write it and you have written CCR0, including its 16-bit width guard."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: shadow register</dt>
+    <dd>A shadow (like Timer_B's TBxCLn compare latches — still unmodeled) is genuinely <em>two</em> stores with a defined hand-off moment. An alias is one store. The old model's PERIOD was an accidental shadow with no hand-off at all: two stores that never synchronised — the worst of both designs, and the double-store bug class in a sentence.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="stored-state-modeling">
+  <h2>Stored-state modeling</h2>
+  <div class="pron">state changes only when a command changes it</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>A simulation style in which the device is a validated state machine with no autonomous dynamics: registers hold what was written, queries read them back, and nothing evolves between commands because time does not pass. Configuration is modeled fully; time-driven behaviour (counting, event latching, interrupt generation) is documented as out of scope rather than faked.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>The whole Timer_A/B model: COUNT is "a static register" (audit wording) that never increments; TAIE is stored but never fires; the CAP bit persists but no edge ever latches CCRn. a20fbb1's comments make the boundary explicit — "time does not pass in this simulation" appears in both <code>TimerState</code> and <code>_timer_cc_mode</code>.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"Under stored-state modeling your test asserts that firmware <em>configured</em> the capture unit, then injects the captured value itself — it never waits for one."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: behavioural / cycle-based simulation</dt>
+    <dd>A behavioural simulator advances a clock: counters tick, PWM edges appear, captures latch. Stored-state modeling trades all of that for simplicity and determinism, keeping only the state-space contract (widths, encodings, aliases, resets). The discipline is honesty at the boundary: store and validate everything static, refuse to invent anything dynamic.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
